---
/** Control Hub: Operator Plans Were Built for Phones, Not for Your Business */
---

<section class="sec sec--white">
  <div class="wrap prob">
    <div class="rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">The plan problem</p>
      <h2 class="h2">Operator Plans Were Built for Phones, <em class="s">Not for Your Business</em></h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:22px">Carriers sell a few fixed handset tiers on monthly cycles. Once you sell connectivity to devices, resellers, and enterprise fleets, that model starts working against you.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prob-list rv">
      <div class="prob-item"><span class="prob-no">01</span><div><h3>Fixed Tiers, Very Different Devices</h3><p>A kids' watch, a fleet tracker, and a security camera use data in completely different ways, yet operator plans come in the same few phone-sized tiers.</p></div></div>
      <div class="prob-item"><span class="prob-no">02</span><div><h3>Every Resale Layer Needs Its Own Billing</h3><p>Distributors, sub-distributors, and their customers each need their own plans, prices, and prepaid or postpaid terms, on top of your operator cost.</p></div></div>
      <div class="prob-item"><span class="prob-no">03</span><div><h3>Plan Mapping Lives in Spreadsheets</h3><p>Deciding which customer plan runs on which operator plan by hand means overages on some lines and unused allowance on others as usage shifts.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
